---
import DocsLayout from "../../../layouts/DocsLayout.astro";
import { Code } from "astro-expressive-code/components";
---

<DocsLayout title="Projects, Tenants & Domains">
  <h1>Projects, Tenants &amp; Domains</h1>
  <p>
    Identity separates three concepts that older versions conflated into a
    single "tenant". Getting them straight is the key to the whole isolation
    model:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Project</strong> — the isolation boundary. Think of it as a
      Firebase project: one user pool, one set of credentials, one JWT issuer.
      Each Project maps onto exactly one storage scope (one shard).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Tenant</strong> — a logical company or team entity
      <em>inside</em> a Project. Tenants form automatically from the email
      domains users sign up with (e.g. <code>acme.com</code>), or a signed-in
      user creates one where the Project allows
      <a href="#self-service-tenants">self-service tenants</a>. A single
      Project can hold many Tenants.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Domain</strong> — a DNS domain a Tenant registers and proves
      it controls. Verifying <code>acme.com</code> binds it to that one
      Tenant, so identity attributes <code>alice@acme.com</code> to it; until
      then the registration is only a claim, and several Tenants may hold
      one.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The Project is the isolation shard</h2>
  <p>
    The Project — not the tenant — is the unit of physical isolation. All
    per-request resolution starts by resolving the Project (the shard), either
    by API credential (<code>public_id</code> on a publishable / secret /
    mTLS credential) or by the <code>Host</code> header matching a configured
    project auth-domain. The user's Tenants are resolved <em>afterward</em>,
    from their memberships, and never select the Project. A token's
    <code>tenant</code> claim is the deployment default
    (<code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID</code>) unless the client
    <a href="#choosing-a-tenant-for-a-token">chooses a tenant</a> on refresh.
  </p>
  <p>
    This inversion means a single B2C product (millions of users, no company
    concept) and a B2B product (many company tenants) use the same model: one
    Project per product, Tenants grow underneath it without re-sharding.
  </p>

  <h2>The user pool is per Project; login is always by email</h2>
  <p>
    Users belong to a <strong>Project</strong>, not a Tenant. Uniqueness is
    <code>(project_id, lower(email))</code> — exactly one identity per person
    per Project, no matter how many company domains they touch. Authentication
    always resolves the identity by <code>(project_id, email)</code> first; the
    user's Tenants are derived afterward from their memberships, a domain one
    counting only while the email is verified and on a domain the tenant has
    verified. The Tenant is never an input to login, and signing in leaves the
    token's <code>tenant</code> claim at the deployment default.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`# A user proves: "I am alice@acme.com in project P"
# identity then derives: "...and acme.com makes her a member of Acme"
PasswordLogin(project=P, email="alice@acme.com", password=...)
  -> identity   (project_id=P, email=alice@acme.com)
  -> membership (Acme, from the verified acme.com domain; see ListMyTenants)
  -> token      ("tenant": GATEWAY_DEFAULT_TENANT_ID until a refresh chooses Acme)`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2>Per-project serving domains</h2>
  <p>
    A Project can serve auth on one or more hostnames
    (<em>project auth-domains</em>). Incoming requests without an explicit
    credential are resolved to a Project by matching the <code>Host</code>
    header against <code>project_auth_domains.hostname</code>. One domain per
    project is marked primary (used to build links such as magic-link and
    OAuth callback URLs). The domain-management RPCs
    (<code>CreateDomain</code>, <code>VerifyDomain</code>,
    <code>AddProjectAuthDomain</code>, <code>SetPrimaryAuthDomain</code>) are
    documented in the API reference.
  </p>

  <h2>One Project per deployment, many on one datastore</h2>
  <p>
    A single Identity container serves one default Project, configured at
    startup. Multiple Projects can share one Postgres instance and stay fully
    isolated — every repository query is scoped by
    <code>WHERE project_id = $1</code>, which is the real isolation boundary and
    is backend-agnostic. On Postgres, row-level security adds defence-in-depth
    on top; the per-query project scope is what guarantees isolation on every
    backend.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`# Two products sharing one Postgres, isolated by Project
# Product A
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://.../identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_product_a \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0

# Product B
docker run ... \\
  -e GATEWAY_REPO_DRIVER=postgres \\
  -e GATEWAY_POSTGRES_DSN=postgres://.../identity \\
  -e GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ID=proj_product_b \\
  ghcr.io/elloloop/identity:1.2.0`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2 id="self-service-tenants">Self-service tenants</h2>
  <p>
    A Project can let its signed-in users create tenants of their own, for a
    team that has no company domain behind it. It is off by
    default; the Project turns it on in its <code>config_json</code> (the
    default Project takes it from
    <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_ENABLED</code> when a request falls
    through to it; one that reaches the default Project by its own project
    key or auth hostname reads that Project's <code>config_json</code>
    instead):
  </p>
  <Code code={`{ "tenants": { "self_service": true } }`} lang="json" />
  <p>
    <code>CreateTenant(name)</code> then creates a claimed tenant and makes the
    caller its <code>owner</code>, in one transaction. A caller who has not
    verified their email is refused with <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. Each user may create at
    most <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_MAX_PER_USER</code> tenants (default
    10) per Project in <code>GATEWAY_SELF_SERVICE_TENANTS_WINDOW_SECONDS</code>
    (default one day); past that the call is refused with
    <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>. With the setting off it is
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>. A retried call can create a second
    tenant, so reconcile with <code>ListMyTenants</code>, which returns every
    claimed tenant the caller is an active member of, with their role. A
    membership that came from the email's domain is listed only while that
    email is verified and on a domain the tenant has verified. A self-service
    tenant cannot be renamed or deleted yet, and a member cannot leave one.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant admin registers and verifies email domains with
    <code>CreateDomain</code> and <code>VerifyDomain</code>. A tenant may make
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_DOMAIN_ATTEMPTS_MAX_PER_TENANT</code> of them
    together (default 100) in
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_DOMAIN_ATTEMPTS_WINDOW_SECONDS</code> (default one
    day), and both share a per-IP budget
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_DOMAIN_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window) on
    the HTTP surface: over it a call gets HTTP 429 with a
    <code>Retry-After</code> header, which Connect clients surface as
    <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. A host serving identity over native gRPC
    applies its own. A registered domain captures no signups until it is
    verified, and a failed re-check never un-verifies it. Registering a
    domain another tenant has verified is <code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code>, so a
    tenant admin can learn which domains are onboarded in the Project.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant is administered only while it is claimed or latent. Once an
    operator suspends it, its owners and admins are refused with
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> by every domain and membership call
    (registering, verifying and listing domains; letting a domain's people
    join; inviting; listing invitations and members; removing, suspending
    or restoring a member; setting a member's role; transferring
    ownership), and an invitation to it
    can no longer be
    accepted, nor can anyone join it by domain. Nothing they had set up is
    changed.
  </p>
  <p>
    An owner hands the tenant on with
    <code>TransferTenantOwnership(tenant_id, user_id)</code>: the member
    named becomes an owner and the caller an admin, in one step, so the
    tenant is never left without an owner. Naming a co-owner leaves them an
    owner and the caller an admin, which is how an owner steps down. Only an
    owner may do it (<code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> otherwise), to an active
    member (<code>NOT_FOUND</code> otherwise; a member through their email's
    domain only while the tenant verifies that domain) who could create a
    tenant themselves: an active, signed-up person with a verified email
    (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> otherwise). The last owner cannot be
    removed, even by two removals at once or a removal racing a transfer, so
    a tenant changes hands this way. The transfer is audited as
    <code>tenant_ownership_transferred</code>. A transfer is not idempotent:
    once it succeeds the caller is an admin, so a retry is
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>; a client that lost the response reads
    <code>ListTenantMembers</code> to see where ownership stands. An admin
    can still invite an owner, themselves included, so being the owner does
    not yet keep a tenant from its admins (tracked in
    <a href="https://github.com/elloloop/identity/issues/657">issue #657,
    admins can make themselves owners</a>).
  </p>
  <h3 id="joining-by-domain">Joining by domain</h3>
  <p>
    A signup with a verified email joins its domain's tenant through
    auto-formation, but only at signup, so some people on a verified domain
    are in no tenant of it: people left in the domain's latent tenant when a
    different tenant verified the domain, people whose email moved onto the
    domain after they signed up, and people whose membership ended. An
    owner or admin can let such people join themselves, domain by domain,
    with <code>SetDomainJoin(domain_id, allow_join)</code>; it is off until
    they turn it on, and the <code>Domain</code> it returns carries
    <code>allow_join</code>. A signed-in person then finds the tenant their
    verified email's domain lets them join and joins it as a member through
    the domain, a membership that ends when the email leaves the domain:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`ListJoinableTenants()
  -> tenants: [{ tenant_id, name }]   # at most one; empty if none, already a member, removed or suspended
JoinTenantByDomain(tenant_id)
  -> membership { role: "member", source: "domain", status: "active" }
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED     # not the tenant your domain lets you join, whether it exists or not,
                           # or you were removed from it or are suspended in it
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION   # your email is not verified`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    If you are already a member, as <code>ListMyTenants</code> counts it,
    the join returns your membership as it is, even after joining has been
    turned off, and records nothing. Turning joining off ends no
    membership. A member an owner or admin removes with
    <code>RemoveTenantMember</code> cannot join again by domain: the tenant
    is no longer joinable for them and the join is
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>, until an invitation they accept, or an
    operator's grant, lets them back in. A membership through the domain
    that ends because the email left it is not a removal, so its holder may
    join again on returning. A change of the setting is audited as
    <code>domain_join_set</code>, a join as
    <code>tenant_joined_by_domain</code>, and a removal as
    <code>tenant_member_removed</code>.
  </p>
  <p id="domain-join-sign-up">
    A project whose <code>access.mode</code> is <code>invite</code> refuses
    self-signup, so a person on such a domain with no account yet cannot
    reach <code>JoinTenantByDomain</code> unless the project sets
    <code>access.admit_domain_join</code> (off by default; for the
    env-configured default project,
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ADMIT_DOMAIN_JOIN</code>). Then a sign-in
    that proves the address, through an OAuth provider that verified it or a
    redeemed emailed code or link, creates the account when the address's
    domain is one the rule above would let it join by, and an emailed code is
    sent to such an address.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Only a tenant not made through self-service <code>CreateTenant</code>
      counts, as for invited sign-up: anyone could make a tenant and verify a
      domain they own. A public mail domain never counts.
    </li>
    <li>
      A password sign-up, a passkey sign-up and the anonymous-account upgrade
      are still refused, as is every address the deny layer turns away.
      <code>accounts.email_signup</code> set to <code>admin</code> or
      <code>off</code>, and <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false</code>
      for codes and links, still close it, and no code is sent then.
    </li>
    <li>
      The account joins no tenant, and forms none from its domain, until it
      calls <code>JoinTenantByDomain</code>. Turning joining off afterwards
      leaves an account that signs in but belongs to no tenant.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each admission is audited as <code>signup_admitted_by_domain_join</code>
      once the account exists, naming the tenant and the domain.
    </li>
    <li>Postgres only: the memory and SQLite drivers keep no tenant domains.</li>
  </ul>
  <h3 id="suspending-a-member">Suspending a member</h3>
  <p>
    An owner or admin who needs to stop someone acting in the tenant for a
    while, without losing their role or how they joined, suspends them
    rather than removing them, and restores them later:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`SuspendTenantMember(tenant_id, user_id)
  -> membership { status: "suspended", role, source }   # also if already suspended
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT      # user_id is you; remove yourself to leave
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION   # the tenant's last active owner
  -> NOT_FOUND             # not an active or suspended member
RestoreTenantMember(tenant_id, user_id)
  -> membership { status: "active", role, source }      # also if already active
  -> membership { status: "inactive", ... }             # a domain member now off the tenant's verified domains
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT      # user_id is you
  -> NOT_FOUND             # not an active or suspended member`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    Both need an active owner or admin of a tenant that is not suspended
    (<code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> otherwise). A restore checks again, as
    it writes, that the caller still holds an active owner or admin
    membership, so an owner or admin suspended while their call is in flight
    lifts nothing. An admin may suspend an owner while another active owner
    remains. While suspended, the member is listed
    by <code>ListTenantMembers</code> with status <code>suspended</code>, is
    left out of <code>ListMyTenants</code> and
    <code>ListJoinableTenants</code>, is refused the tenant at a refresh
    (<code>NOT_A_MEMBER</code>), and cannot administer it. Nor can they come
    back on their own: accepting an invitation to the tenant is
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> and leaves the invitation open, and so is
    <code>JoinTenantByDomain</code>. A suspended membership through the
    email's domain does not end when the email leaves the domain, so leaving
    and returning cannot shed it; if the email is off the tenant's verified
    domains when the member is restored, the membership ends then instead,
    and they come back as any former member does: through an invitation, or
    by joining once their email is on a domain that lets people join. An
    operator's <code>AdminAddTenantAdmin</code> or
    <code>AdminRecoverTenantOwnership</code> also lifts a suspension, and
    audits the lift as a restore with the operator's actor: the
    <code>tenant_operator</code> credential, or none for the admin
    secret.
  </p>
  <p>
    A suspension takes effect at the member's next refresh. It ends none of
    their sessions and revokes none of the agent grants they made, and an
    access token that already names the tenant works until it expires. A
    suspension is audited as <code>tenant_member_suspended</code> and a
    restore as <code>tenant_member_restored</code>, each with the
    membership's role, source and resulting status; a call that changes
    nothing records nothing. A removal's <code>tenant_member_removed</code>
    records the status the membership had, so removing a suspended member
    shows as one.
  </p>
  <h3 id="setting-a-members-role">Setting a member's role</h3>
  <p>
    An owner or admin makes a member an admin, or an admin a member:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`SetTenantMemberRole(tenant_id, user_id, role)   # role: "admin" | "member"
  -> membership { role, status, source }   # also if the member already holds role
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT      # a missing id, or a role other than "admin" or "member"; "owner" moves by TransferTenantOwnership
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION   # user_id is an owner; a promotion of an account that is not an active, signed-up person's with a verified email; or no active owner or admin would be left
  -> NOT_FOUND             # not an active or suspended member; a domain member off the tenant's verified domains
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED     # not an active owner or admin of tenant_id, or the tenant is suspended`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    The caller may name themselves, so an admin steps down, but a tenant
    with no active owner keeps at least one active admin. The check is made
    where the change is written, holding the memberships of the tenant's
    owners and admins, so two admins stepping down at once cannot both
    succeed, and a caller suspended or made a member while their call is in
    flight changes nothing. An admin may change another admin's role, as an
    admin may suspend or remove one; an owner's role changes only by
    <code>TransferTenantOwnership</code>. A promotion needs an account that
    could own a tenant, since an admin sends the tenant's invitations, so an
    agent account is never made an admin. A suspended member keeps the
    suspension with the new role. A member through their email's domain made
    an admin becomes an <code>added</code> member, as a transfer makes an
    owner, so the role does not end when the email leaves the domain.
  </p>
  <p>
    The role counts at once. An access token names the tenant, not the
    holder's role in it, so nothing waits for a refresh:
    <code>ListMyTenants</code>, <code>ListTenantMembers</code> and every
    call that needs an owner or admin read the membership as it stands. A
    change is audited as <code>tenant_member_role_changed</code>, with the
    new role, the one it replaced (<code>previous_role</code>), and the
    membership's source and status; setting the role a member holds records
    nothing.
  </p>

  <h2 id="choosing-a-tenant-for-a-token">Choosing a tenant for a token</h2>
  <p>
    An access token's <code>tenant</code> claim is the deployment default
    unless the client asks for another on refresh. Selection is off by
    default; a server turns it on with
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_SELECTION_ENABLED</code>, on the Postgres driver
    only:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`RefreshToken(refresh_token=..., tenant_id="3f9c2a7b5e1d4c8fa06b9e2d7c41f5a3")
  -> access token with "tenant": "3f9c2a7b..."  # an active member the tenant's login policy admits
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED, detail NOT_A_MEMBER     # not a member, or not claimed; token unspent
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED, detail LOGIN_POLICY     # the tenant's login policy refuses this session; token unspent
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION, detail PHONE_VERIFICATION_REQUIRED  # the tenant requires a verified phone; token unspent
  -> UNAVAILABLE, detail UNAVAILABLE            # membership or policy unreadable; token unspent
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION, detail NOT_ENABLED    # selection is off on this server; token unspent
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT, no detail                # not a tenant id at all; token not read`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    A member only through their email's domain must have verified that email
    and still be on a domain the tenant has verified; moving the email off the
    domain ends the membership, and moving back does not rejoin. A session a
    <a href="/identity/docs/auth/recovery-emails">recovery email</a> opened
    (its token's <code>amr</code> names <code>recovery_email</code>) is never
    such a member: it selects a tenant only through an explicit membership. Every refused
    selection carries a <code>TenantSelectionRefusedDetails</code> error
    detail and consumes nothing, so a client that finds the detail refreshes
    again without <code>tenant_id</code>. <code>RefreshToken</code> refuses an
    account over its own state with the same codes and no such detail, and
    that refusal may have spent the token, so retry only when the detail is
    present. A <code>tenant_id</code> longer than 64 characters, or holding
    anything but ASCII letters, digits, <code>-</code> and <code>_</code>, is
    <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code> before the refresh token is read. Each refresh chooses again; one without
    <code>tenant_id</code> returns to the default, and the client reads the
    tenant it got from the token's <code>tenant</code> claim. Identity's HTTP
    surface checks the tenant's status through a short cache, so suspending a
    tenant refuses its tokens there within
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_STATUS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code> (default 30); ending
    or suspending a membership takes effect at the next
    refresh, within the access-token lifetime. That holds in
    <code>mode=session</code> too: removing or suspending a member ends none of their
    sessions, so a token that already carries the tenant stays valid until it
    expires. A service that verifies tokens
    itself, as a host serving identity over native gRPC does, must accept a
    non-default <code>tenant</code> before its clients select one, and sees a
    suspension only when the token expires unless it checks the tenant too.
  </p>
  <p>
    The claim asserts membership, and that the tenant's login policy admitted
    the session when the token was issued. <code>role</code> stays the
    account's role and is never a role in the chosen tenant (read that from
    <code>ListMyTenants</code>). A policy that limits how its users sign in
    (SSO required, 2FA required, or a list of allowed methods) admits only a
    session it governed at sign-in, one whose account's email is on a domain
    the tenant has verified. A session does not record how it signed in, so a
    member from another domain is refused with <code>LOGIN_POLICY</code>
    even if they did sign in through the tenant's SSO; the tenant can invite
    them on its own domain instead. The policy's session timeouts apply to
    every selection, and a session past them is refused the same way.
  </p>

  <h2>Invitations</h2>
  <p>
    A tenant owner or admin invites by email with
    <code>CreateTenantInvitation</code>. Every invitation sends an email, so
    each is bounded: per client IP
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window),
    per tenant (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_TENANT</code>, default
    100, in <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_WINDOW_SECONDS</code>, default one
    day), per inviter across their tenants
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_INVITER</code>, default 100), per
    tenant and recipient
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_MAX_PER_RECIPIENT</code>, default 10, each
    tenant counting its own), and per tenant and recipient by a resend cooldown
    (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_RESEND_COOLDOWN_SECONDS</code>, default
    300). Past any of these but the per-IP one the call is
    <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>; past the per-IP one, which applies on the
    HTTP surface only, it gets HTTP 429 with a <code>Retry-After</code>
    header, which Connect clients surface as <code>UNAVAILABLE</code>. The email names an operator's or a domain
    tenant, but never a self-service tenant, whose name its creator typed.
    The invitation is stored first and the email sent after, once and
    synchronously, in the same call; a failed send does not fail the call.
    The response's <code>email_send_status</code> says what became of that
    attempt: <code>SENT</code> when a configured mail provider accepted the
    email (not proof it reached the inbox); <code>FAILED</code> when no
    provider accepted it, the mail settings name no valid provider, or the
    email could not be built (an invalid sender address, say); and
    <code>NOT_CONFIGURED</code> when the server has no mail settings at all,
    the one case <code>raw_token</code> carries the token to hand over
    out-of-band. After <code>FAILED</code>, call
    <code>CreateTenantInvitation</code> again once the resend cooldown has
    passed: that revokes the open invitation and sends a new one. The status
    is not stored; record it if you need it later.
    Accepting an invitation needs a verified email at the invited address
    (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> until then; the invitation stays open),
    and never lowers a role, so it cannot demote an owner. A member who is
    suspended in the tenant cannot accept one
    (<code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>; the invitation stays open).
  </p>
  <p>
    An owner or admin withdraws a pending invitation, and its link stops
    working at once:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`RevokeTenantInvitation(tenant_id, invitation_id)
  -> invitation { status: "revoked" }   # also if already revoked; one marked expired comes back "expired"
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION   # accepted: remove the member instead
  -> NOT_FOUND             # not one of tenant_id's invitations, another tenant's or nobody's
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED     # not an active owner or admin of tenant_id, or the tenant is suspended`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    A revocation is audited as <code>tenant_invitation_revoked</code>; a call
    that changes nothing records nothing. A revoke never overwrites an
    invitation accepted before it, but an accept already under way, one that
    read the invitation as pending before the revoke landed, still completes
    and leaves the invitation accepted and its invitee a member. A successful
    revoke therefore does not prove that nobody joined: read
    <code>ListTenantMembers</code> where that matters.
  </p>
  <p>
    The email links to the page set in
    <code>GATEWAY_TENANT_INVITATION_PAGE_URL</code> when one is set, otherwise
    to <code>/auth/accept-invitation?token=…</code> on the project's primary
    auth domain, else on <code>GATEWAY_APP_BASE_URL</code> (see
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration#tenant-invitation-links">tenant
    invitation links</a>). That page, which your app serves, signs the invitee
    in and calls <code>AcceptTenantInvitation</code> with the token.
  </p>
  <p id="reading-an-invitation">
    Before anyone signs in, the page can read the invitation with
    <code>GetTenantInvitation(token)</code>, which needs no session and changes
    nothing: an expired invitation reads <code>expired</code> without being
    marked so, and the token is not used up. It answers the tenant's id and
    name (the name the email shows, so empty for a self-service tenant), the
    inviter's name and email, the invited address, the role, the expiry
    (<code>expires_at_ms</code>), the status (<code>pending</code>,
    <code>expired</code>, <code>accepted</code> or <code>revoked</code>) and
    <code>account_exists</code>: whether an account of the project holds the
    address, as its email or as a recovery email. A server with no mail
    settings hands the token to the inviter, so it never answers
    <code>account_exists</code> (always false), and the invitation records
    that its token was handed out, so it is never answered for that
    invitation, or one stored before the server recorded this (schema 67),
    even once the server has mail settings: the answer would tell an inviter
    whether any address they invite has an account. A token no
    invitation of the project has is <code>NOT_FOUND</code>, and a blank one
    <code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code>. Calls are limited per client IP
    (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_VERIFY_PER_IP</code>, default 20 per window, a
    budget of their own, shared by HTTP and native gRPC). Send it as a POST,
    so this call puts the token in no URL; the response is
    <code>Cache-Control: no-store</code>. The emailed link does carry the
    token in its URL, and the token can create the account, so the page it
    opens should send <code>Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code>, take the
    token out of its URL once it has read it
    (<code>history.replaceState</code>), and load no third-party resources.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`GetTenantInvitation(token)
  -> { tenant_id, tenant_name, inviter_name, inviter_email, email, role,
       expires_at_ms, status, account_exists }
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT              # a blank token
  -> NOT_FOUND                     # no invitation of the project has the token
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED                 # the memory or SQLite driver`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p id="invited-sign-up">
    An invitee with no account yet signs up on that page. A project whose
    <code>access.mode</code> is <code>invite</code> refuses self-signup, so it
    creates that account only when it sets
    <code>access.admit_tenant_invitations</code> (off by default; for the
    env-configured default project,
    <code>GATEWAY_DEFAULT_PROJECT_ADMIT_TENANT_INVITATIONS</code>). Then a
    sign-in that proves the address, through an OAuth provider that verified it
    or a redeemed emailed code or link, creates the account when a pending,
    unexpired invitation names it, and an emailed code is sent to such an
    address.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Only invitations from a claimed or latent tenant that was not made through
      self-service <code>CreateTenant</code> count, so a person who makes a
      tenant cannot invite others into the project. Tenants formed from an
      email domain and claimed by DNS verification do count: their owners and
      admins decide who gets in. The inviter must still be an active owner or
      admin of the tenant, with an account that can sign in and is a person.
    </li>
    <li>
      <code>PasswordSignup</code>, a passkey sign-up and the anonymous-account
      upgrade, none of which proves the address, are still refused (a password
      account comes only from the invitation's own link, below), as is every
      address the deny layer turns away. <code>accounts.email_signup</code> set to <code>admin</code>
      or <code>off</code>, and <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORDLESS_SIGNUP_ENABLED=false</code>
      for codes and links, still close it, and no code is sent then.
    </li>
    <li>
      The account joins no tenant until it accepts the invitation, not even the
      tenant that verified its email domain. Revoking or expiring the invitation
      afterwards leaves an account that signs in but belongs to no tenant;
      suspend or delete it to remove access.
    </li>
    <li>
      Each admission is audited as <code>signup_admitted_by_invitation</code>
      once the account exists, naming the invitation, tenant and inviter.
    </li>
    <li>
      Postgres only: the memory and SQLite drivers keep no tenant invitations.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p id="invitation-link-sign-up">
    The invitation's own link proves the address too, where it was emailed.
    With the same opt-in, <code>SignUpWithTenantInvitation(token, name,
    password)</code> creates the account the invitation names, verified and
    active, with that name and password, and returns its session as
    <code>PasswordSignup</code> does. It needs no session and is gated as
    <code>PasswordSignup</code> is: <code>GATEWAY_AUTH_ALLOW_LOCAL</code>,
    <code>GATEWAY_PASSWORD_SIGNUP_ENABLED</code>, the
    <code>X-Assurance-Token</code> header while
    <code>GATEWAY_ASSURANCE_ENFORCE_PASSWORD_SIGNUP</code> is on, and the
    sign-up budget per client IP (<code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_SIGNUP_PER_IP</code>,
    shared with <code>PasswordSignup</code> and <code>UsernameSignup</code>,
    and spent over HTTP and native gRPC alike).
    It does not accept the invitation: the account joins no tenant, not even
    its email domain's, and the invitation stays pending until the account
    calls <code>AcceptTenantInvitation</code>, which its new session can do at
    once.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      The token's own invitation must count as above: pending and unexpired,
      from a tenant not made through self-service, whose inviter is still an
      active owner or admin who can sign in. Another invitation to the same
      address does not stand in for it.
    </li>
    <li>
      A server with no mail settings hands the token to the inviter, so there
      the call is always refused, and so is a project that has not opted in:
      both <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> with the stable
      <code>invitation_signup_unavailable</code> token, before the token is
      looked up. Each invitation records whether its token went only into its
      email, so the token of one created while the server had no mail
      settings, or stored before the server recorded this (schema 67),
      creates no account even once the server has them: the same refusal.
      Its invitee still accepts it after a sign-in that proves the address,
      which creates the account here; invite the address again once mail is
      configured to send them a link that creates it.
    </li>
    <li>
      An invitation that was accepted, revoked or has expired is
      <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>; one that does not count, or an address
      the deny layer refuses, <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>; an address an
      account holds, as its email or a recovery email,
      <code>ALREADY_EXISTS</code> (the person signs in instead); a token no
      invitation has <code>NOT_FOUND</code>.
    </li>
    <li>
      The password meets the deployment's rules and the address's tenant's
      password policy (<code>INVALID_ARGUMENT</code> otherwise), and a login
      policy for the address that requires single sign-on or two-factor
      authentication refuses the sign-up (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>),
      as one that does not allow passwords does
      (<code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>). The name is required, at most 100
      characters, with no control or invisible formatting characters.
    </li>
    <li>
      The admission is audited as <code>signup_admitted_by_invitation</code>
      naming the token's invitation, and the sign-in as
      <code>login_success</code> with method
      <code>tenant_invitation_signup</code>.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <Code
    code={`curl -sX POST https://auth.example.com/identity.v1.IdentityService/SignUpWithTenantInvitation \\
  -H 'Content-Type: application/json' \\
  -H "X-Assurance-Token: $ASSURANCE_TOKEN" \\
  -d '{ "token": "<the token on the link>", "name": "Ravi Kumar", "password": "Corr3ct!Horse" }'
# -> { "user": { "id": "...", "email": "ravi@acme.example", "emailVerified": true, ... },
#      "accessToken": "eyJ...", "refreshToken": "...", "expiresIn": 900 }`}
    lang="bash"
  />

  <h2 id="managed-accounts">Managed accounts</h2>
  <p>
    A tenant may give its people accounts on a domain verified for it, and
    manage them, as a workspace administrator does: an owner or admin creates
    <code>ann@acme.example.com</code> before Ann ever signs in, and the account
    belongs to that tenant only. Personal accounts and invitations by email are
    unchanged. Managed accounts are off unless the server sets
    <code>GATEWAY_MANAGED_ACCOUNTS_ENABLED</code> (postgres only, and only with
    <code>GATEWAY_REVOCATION_MODE=session</code>) and the project sets
    <code>access.admit_managed_accounts</code> in its <code>config_json</code>;
    a tenant made through self-service <code>CreateTenant</code> also needs
    <code>access.admit_managed_accounts_self_service</code>. The switches gate
    new writes only: an account that is already managed keeps every
    restriction below.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`CreateTenantInvitation(tenant_id, email: "ann@example.net", role,
                       account_username: "ann", account_domain: "acme.example.com",
                       display_name: "Ann Lee")
  -> invitation { is_managed_setup: true, account_address: "ann@acme.example.com",
                  user_id, email: "ann@example.net", ... }, email_send_status
  -> ALREADY_EXISTS       MANAGED_BY_THIS_TENANT   # already one of this tenant's accounts, set up
  -> ALREADY_EXISTS       ADDRESS_UNAVAILABLE      # held outside the tenant; which holder is not said
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION  DOMAIN_UNVERIFIED        # the domain is not verified for tenant_id
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION  POLICY                   # not admitted, the project's account domain, or denied
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION  email_delivery_disabled  # the server or project sends no mail
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED    # not an owner or admin; an owner role, or replacing an owner's link, needs an owner`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    Each refusal carries a <code>ManagedAccountRefusedDetails</code> with its
    reason. <code>email</code> is only where the set-up link goes; the
    account's address is
    <code>&lt;account_username&gt;@&lt;account_domain&gt;</code>, and
    <code>display_name</code> (plain text, at most 100 characters) is its name.
    The account is created waiting to be set up, with no password, and the
    link is mailed: its token goes only into the email, so a server without
    mail refuses. Asking again for one of the tenant's accounts that has not
    been set up sends a new link, to the new <code>email</code> if it changed,
    and the earlier link stops working; the name stays as it was. The
    invitation limits and the resend cooldown apply, with the account's
    address as the recipient; the address the link is sent to is held to the
    cooldown and to the per-recipient cap too, counted across the project.
    An ordinary invitation to one of the tenant's
    managed addresses is refused with <code>MANAGED_BY_THIS_TENANT</code>; one
    to another tenant's managed address is sent as any invitation, so no tenant
    learns which addresses others manage, and acceptance refuses it. Creation
    and a resend are audited as <code>managed_account_created</code> and
    <code>managed_account_setup_resent</code> (with whether the delivery
    address changed), with no address in the trail.
  </p>
  <p id="checking-managed-addresses">
    Before creating accounts, an owner or admin can ask which addresses are
    usable. The answer is a point in time: creation decides again.
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`CheckManagedAddresses(tenant_id, addresses: [{ username, domain }, ...])   # 1 to 100
  -> results, in order: { address, status }
       FREE                     # no account or recovery email holds it
       MANAGED_BY_THIS_TENANT   # already one of this tenant's accounts
       ADDRESS_UNAVAILABLE      # held outside the tenant; which holder is not said
       DOMAIN_UNVERIFIED        # not a domain verified for tenant_id; nothing looked up
       INVALID                  # not a valid username or address, or one the project refuses
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION POLICY   # managed accounts not admitted for the tenant
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED            # not an owner or admin of tenant_id, or the tenant is suspended
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT             # no tenant_id, no addresses, or more than 100
  -> RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED           # past the tenant's or the client IP's budget`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    Every lookup is confined to the request's project and the caller's
    tenant: only addresses on domains verified for that tenant are looked up,
    against that project's accounts and recovery emails, so no tenant learns
    anything about another tenant's domain. Calls are limited per client IP on
    a budget of their own, the size of
    <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_INVITATION_PER_IP</code>, and per tenant by
    <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_MANAGED_ADDRESS_CHECK_PER_TENANT</code> (default
    60 calls per rate-limit window, counted per replica; it cannot be switched
    off). Past either the call is <code>RESOURCE_EXHAUSTED</code>.
  </p>
  <p id="managed-account-set-up">
    <code>GetTenantInvitation</code> marks a set-up link
    <code>is_managed_setup</code>, with <code>email</code> the account's
    address and <code>account_exists</code> false; while the link is pending
    and is the account's current one it also answers
    <code>account_address</code> and <code>display_name</code> (withheld for a
    self-service tenant, as its name is). It never answers where the link was
    delivered, nor the account's id: anyone holding the token may read it. Clients then show a set-up form that
    asks only for a password, and call
    <code>SignUpWithTenantInvitation(token, password)</code>: the name is the
    tenant's. That needs local auth and mail, but neither password sign-up nor
    the project's invitation sign-up; the password meets the rules, and the
    sign-in the login policy, of the address's tenant, and the deny layer and
    the project's sign-in rules apply. In one transaction the account becomes
    active with its email verified, joins the tenant with the link's role, and
    the link is accepted; then it signs in. A membership an owner suspended
    stays suspended: the password is set, nothing signs in, and the call is
    <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>. A replaced, revoked, used or expired link,
    or a domain no longer verified, is <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>; a
    link whose issuer is no longer an owner or admin of the tenant, or an
    account removed from it, is <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>. The
    first set-up emits <code>user.created</code>, and every set-up is audited
    as <code>managed_account_set_up</code>.
    <code>AcceptTenantInvitation</code> never redeems a set-up link: once it
    is used, it returns its own account's membership and refuses anyone else.
  </p>
  <p id="managed-account-sign-in">
    A managed account holds a session only while it is active, has been set
    up, its membership of the tenant that manages it is active, and that
    tenant is claimed or latent (a suspended tenant's accounts sign nothing
    in). The same rule decides whether it may stand behind an agent: an agent
    it owns gets no session, refresh, OAuth or machine exchange, and no
    teammate-key token, while its owner, or the person who granted a
    teammate's hire, is out of standing, and gets them again once that person
    is restored. A standing that cannot be read refuses the token and leaves
    the grant in place for a retry. Every way
    of signing in, a two-step code and a refresh included, checks this when it
    mints tokens, whether or not <code>GATEWAY_MANAGED_ACCOUNTS_ENABLED</code>
    is on, and refuses with <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code> otherwise. So an
    owner or admin who suspends or removes a managed member stops the
    account's next sign-in and its next refresh, which spends the refresh
    token; restoring the membership lets it sign in again. Access tokens
    already issued last until they expire. When the membership cannot be read
    the account is refused too, and a refresh keeps its token for a retry.
    Personal accounts are never read for. A managed account's access token
    carries the claim <code>managed: true</code>, a JSON boolean; a personal
    account's token has no <code>managed</code> claim. The claim says what the
    account was when the token was minted: the account's record decides.
  </p>
  <p id="managed-account-refusals">
    A managed account is the tenant's, so what only a person's own account
    may do is refused to it with <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code>
    (<code>managed_account</code>), each logged as
    <code>managed_account_refused</code> with the path: creating a tenant,
    joining one by domain, accepting any invitation, leaving its tenant,
    changing its email, adding a recovery email (<code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>
    with <code>POLICY</code>: its tenant recovers it with a reset), linking a
    provider, deleting itself, owning an agent
    (created for it, offered to it, accepted by it or reassigned to it), and
    merging with another account on either side. It signs in through no
    OAuth provider, since no tenant owns one yet: a provider account at the
    same address would otherwise control it. While it waits to be set up it is
    sent no sign-in code, link or password reset (the request answers as for
    any address), and one already sent signs nothing in and sets nothing. The admin
    <code>DeactivateUser</code>, <code>ReactivateUser</code>,
    <code>DeleteUser</code>, <code>ResetUserPassword</code> and
    <code>UpdateUser</code> refuse it too, and SCIM may read it but not
    replace, patch or delete it (<code>403</code>): its tenant manages it. A
    purge ends
    its set-up links first, the stored-email repair leaves it alone, and it
    forms no tenant from its domain.
  </p>
  <p>
    <code>RevokeTenantInvitation</code> on a set-up link also deletes its
    account while that account has never been set up, whatever the link's
    status and whatever the managed-account switches say, which frees the
    address and is audited as <code>managed_account_deleted</code>. It emits
    no <code>user.deleted</code>: an account never set up was never announced
    with <code>user.created</code>, which comes at its first set-up. Only an
    owner may revoke an owner's link. A used link is
    <code>FAILED_PRECONDITION</code>: remove the member instead.
    <code>ListTenantInvitations</code> shows each link's
    <code>is_managed_setup</code>, <code>account_address</code> and
    <code>user_id</code>, and <code>ListTenantMembers</code> each member's
    <code>account_kind</code>, <code>PERSONAL</code> or <code>MANAGED</code>.
    A deployment that keeps its own list of pending invitations should keep
    the set-up record (account address and delivery address), so its waiting
    list survives a link's expiry.
  </p>

  <h2 id="operating-tenants">Operating tenants</h2>
  <p>
    A platform operator reads a Project's tenants with the control-plane
    admin calls, which authenticate with the <code>X-Admin-Secret</code>
    header (<code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code>) rather than a user token,
    or with a <a href="#tenant-operator-credential">credential for one
    Project's tenants</a>. They return the tenant's own record, never its
    members, domains or policy:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminGetTenant(project_id, tenant_id)
  -> { id, name, primary_domain, status, created_by, created_at, updated_at }
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT              # a blank project_id or tenant_id
  -> NOT_FOUND                     # the Project holds no such tenant
AdminListTenants(project_id, limit=50, cursor="")
  -> { tenants: [...], next_cursor }   # every status, newest first
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT              # a blank project_id, or a cursor that does not decode
Both:
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED                 # GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET is unset and the call
                                   #   presents X-Admin-Secret or no
                                   #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED             # a wrong X-Admin-Secret, or a refused
                                   #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    <code>limit</code> defaults to 50 and is capped at 200. Pass each page's
    <code>next_cursor</code> to read the next; it is empty on the last page.
    A cursor holds the last tenant's position, not an offset, so paging never
    repeats or skips a tenant that existed when it began; a tenant created
    meanwhile may or may not appear. A cursor is not bound to the Project it
    came from: the page is always of <code>project_id</code>'s tenants alone,
    and a Project with no tenants, or one that does not exist, is an empty
    page.
  </p>
  <p>
    An operator stops a tenant by suspending it, and starts it again by
    restoring it. Each call needs a reason, which the audit trail keeps as
    <code>tenant_suspended</code> or <code>tenant_restored</code>:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminSuspendTenant(project_id, tenant_id, reason="billing dispute")
  -> { tenant: { status: "suspended", ... } }  # was claimed, or already suspended
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION                       # latent: it governs nothing yet
AdminRestoreTenant(project_id, tenant_id, reason="dispute settled")
  -> { tenant: { status: "claimed", ... } }    # was suspended, or already claimed
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION                       # latent, or no creator, owner or admin:
                                               #   appoint an owner with
                                               #   AdminRecoverTenantOwnership first
                                               #   (a credential restores a tenant
                                               #   a credential created)
Both:
  -> ABORTED                                   # its status changed meanwhile; read it again
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT                          # a blank id, or a reason that is blank, over 500
                                               #   characters, or holds a control or formatting
                                               #   character such as a newline
  -> NOT_FOUND                                 # the Project holds no such tenant
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED                             # GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET is unset and
                                               #   the call presents X-Admin-Secret or no
                                               #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED                         # a wrong X-Admin-Secret, or a refused
                                               #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    A suspended tenant keeps its domains, members and invitations. A refresh
    that chooses it is refused, its selected tokens are refused within the
    tenant-status cache TTL (<code>GATEWAY_TENANT_STATUS_CACHE_TTL_SECONDS</code>,
    30 seconds by default), and its owners and admins can no longer
    administer it or transfer its ownership. The agent grants and machines of its members, in any
    membership status, and of the accounts on its verified domains and its
    primary domain are revoked.
  </p>
  <p>
    A suspension never loosens sign-in controls. While the tenant is
    suspended its login policy keeps governing the sign-ins through its
    verified domains: its allowed methods, SSO and 2FA requirements,
    password minimum and session timeouts apply to those accounts as they
    did while it was claimed.
  </p>
  <p>
    Restoring it gives back administration at once and selection within the
    same cache TTL, and leaves sign-in as it was. Like any change of a
    tenant's status, it revokes again the agent grants and machines of the
    same members and accounts, including any connected during the
    suspension; no grant comes back. Asking for the
    status a tenant already has returns it unchanged and records nothing, so a
    retry is safe; only the call that changes the status records its reason.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant whose owners have all been deleted, deactivated or merged away
    has no owner. While one of its admins both can sign in and holds an
    active membership, that admin invites a new owner with
    <code>CreateTenantInvitation</code> and
    <code>role="owner"</code>. Once no owner or admin both can sign in and
    holds an active membership, nobody can govern it, and an operator gives
    it an owner again, with a reason the
    audit trail keeps as <code>tenant_ownership_recovered</code>:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminRecoverTenantOwnership(project_id, tenant_id, user_id, reason="owner left the company")
  -> { membership: { role: "owner", source: "added", ... } }   # an existing invited or created source is kept
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION   # an owner or admin who is a person can still sign in
                           #   and holds an active membership, the account
                           #   cannot own a tenant, or latent
  -> NOT_FOUND             # no such tenant or account
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT      # a blank id, or a reason that is blank, over 500
                           #   characters, or holds a control character
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED         # GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET is unset and the call
                           #   presents X-Admin-Secret or no
                           #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED     # a wrong X-Admin-Secret, or a refused
                           #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    The account may be any person in the Project who could own a tenant,
    member or not, and a suspended tenant can be recovered. While an owner
    or an admin who is a person can still sign in and holds an active
    membership, recovery is refused: an owner transfers, an admin invites. A
    suspended tenant refuses both, so the operator restores it first and
    they act then. So recovery never puts an outside account above people
    who can act. An account pending deletion can still sign in, which
    cancels the deletion, so an owner or admin in that state still counts.
    Naming an account that is already an owner who can sign in, with an
    active membership, returns its membership and records nothing, so a
    retry is safe. A
    refused recovery is not audited. <code>AdminAddTenantAdmin</code>, which
    provisions a tenant's first admins, makes none of these checks when the
    admin secret calls it: the secret can make any account of the Project an
    owner or admin of any of its tenants, lowering an owner included. A
    <code>tenant_operator</code> credential's call is held to recovery's
    rules (below). Each grant is recorded as <code>tenant_admin_added</code>,
    and a suspension it lifts as a restore.
  </p>
  <p>
    Recovery treats a deactivated or suspended account, and an owner or
    admin whose membership of the tenant is suspended, as gone, though each
    can be undone: an owner reactivated after a recovery is an owner again,
    beside the appointed one, so make sure the account will not come back
    first. When every owner is gone and the admins left cannot be reached
    or will not act, a Project admin deactivates their accounts with
    <code>DeactivateUser</code> (audited, and reversible with
    <code>ReactivateUser</code>, but it signs each account out of every
    tenant) and the operator then recovers the tenant.
  </p>

  <h3 id="tenant-operator-credential">A credential for one Project's tenants</h3>
  <p>
    The admin secret governs every Project. A service that runs or
    provisions one Project's tenants can hold a
    <code>tenant_operator</code> credential of that Project instead, which an
    operator mints with the secret. Whoever holds one can also create the
    Project's tenants, appoint their first owners, and add any signed-up
    person of the Project as a member of any of its tenants, including
    tenants with owners of their own, so mint it only for a service trusted
    with that:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminCreateProjectCredential(project_id, kind="tenant_operator")
  -> { credential_id, public_id: "ok_...", raw_key: "ok_....<secret>" }   # raw_key is shown once`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    The service presents <code>raw_key</code> in the
    <code>X-Tenant-Operator-Key</code> header, in place of
    <code>X-Admin-Secret</code>, on nine calls:
    <code>AdminGetTenant</code>, <code>AdminListTenants</code>,
    <code>AdminSuspendTenant</code>, <code>AdminRestoreTenant</code>,
    <code>AdminRecoverTenantOwnership</code>,
    <code>AdminCreateTenant</code>, <code>AdminAddTenantAdmin</code>,
    <code>AdminAddTenantMember</code> and <code>GetLoginPolicy</code>. Each must name the credential's own
    <code>project_id</code>. No other call accepts it: creating Projects or
    credentials, and writing or deleting a login policy, still take the
    admin secret.
  </p>
  <p>
    With it, the service provisions its Project's tenants itself: it creates
    one, claimed at once, makes an account of the Project its first owner or
    admin, and adds the Project's people as members, each recorded with the
    credential as the actor:
  </p>
  <Code
    code={`AdminCreateTenant(project_id, name="Acme", primary_domain="acme.example")
  -> { tenant_id }             # claimed; recorded as tenant_created
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT          # a blank project_id
AdminAddTenantAdmin(project_id, tenant_id, user_id, role="owner")
  -> { membership: { role: "owner", source: "added", ... } }   # recorded as tenant_admin_added;
                               #   asked again, the same membership, recorded once
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION       # an owner or admin who is a person can still sign in
                               #   and holds an active membership, the account
                               #   cannot own a tenant, or latent
  -> NOT_FOUND                 # the tenant or the account is not the Project's
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT          # a blank id, or a role other than owner or admin
AdminAddTenantMember(project_id, tenant_id, user_id)
  -> { membership: { role: "member", source: "added", ... } }  # recorded as tenant_member_added;
                               #   an active member already, of any role: that
                               #   membership, unchanged, recorded nothing
  -> FAILED_PRECONDITION       # the tenant suspended or removed the person, the
                               #   account is not a person with a verified email,
                               #   or latent
  -> NOT_FOUND                 # the tenant or the account is not the Project's
  -> INVALID_ARGUMENT          # a blank id
All three:
  -> UNIMPLEMENTED             # GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET is unset and the call
                               #   presents X-Admin-Secret or no
                               #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key
  -> PERMISSION_DENIED         # a wrong X-Admin-Secret, or a refused
                               #   X-Tenant-Operator-Key`}
    lang="bash"
  />
  <p>
    The credential appoints only a tenant's first governor, under
    recovery's rules: the tenant is claimed or suspended, the account is a
    person who could own a tenant, and nobody who can govern the tenant
    remains. It never overrides or lowers an owner or admin who can act;
    further owners and admins come from the governor it appointed, by
    invitation (an admin it appoints invites the owner). It adds members
    itself, and unlike the appointment, an addition works on a tenant that
    already has its own owners and admins, without their invitation.
    <code>AdminAddTenantMember</code> returns a membership already active
    unchanged, whatever its role and source, so it cannot lower anyone, and
    one through the email's domain stays bound to it. It never lets back in a
    person the tenant suspended or removed, whom only the tenant's owners and
    admins restore or invite. The admin secret's <code>AdminAddTenantMember</code>
    follows the same rules. Either account must already be one of the
    Project's, signed up with a verified email: find its id with
    <code>LookupUsers</code> and a <code>directory_reader</code>
    credential. <code>AdminCreateTenant</code>
    is not idempotent, so keep the <code>tenant_id</code> it returns before
    you retry; a tenant whose grant failed waits, ownerless, for the grant
    to be retried.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant it creates is trusted as one the admin secret creates. Its name
    appears in the tenant's invitation emails, and its primary domain is
    taken as given, unverified: a change of the tenant's status revokes the
    agent grants and machines of the Project's accounts on that domain, as
    for any tenant. Nothing but the per-IP budget below bounds how many
    tenants it creates.
  </p>
  <p>
    A tenant records whether a credential or the admin secret created it.
    Only a <code>tenant_operator</code> credential may restore a suspended
    tenant a credential created while nobody governs it, since the
    credential's holder answers for the tenants it makes. The admin secret
    is held to the old rule even for those tenants, and every other tenant
    with no creator, owner or admin is refused whoever calls: appoint an
    owner first. That includes a tenant created before the record was kept.
    The record is not returned by <code>AdminGetTenant</code>; a refused
    restore means an owner is needed first.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      It is read from the database on every call, so revoking it with
      <code>AdminRevokeProjectCredential</code> holds from the next call, and
      a suspended Project's credential is refused. It works whether or not
      <code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code> is still set. A call that presents
      both headers is checked against the secret alone.
    </li>
    <li>
      What it changes is recorded with the credential as the actor,
      <code>credential:&lt;credential_id&gt;</code>: the
      <code>tenant_created</code>, <code>tenant_admin_added</code>,
      <code>tenant_member_added</code>,
      <code>tenant_suspended</code>, <code>tenant_restored</code> and
      <code>tenant_ownership_recovered</code> records, and the
      <code>tenant_member_restored</code> of a suspension a recovery or a
      grant lifts. With the admin secret those records name no actor.
    </li>
    <li>
      Every refusal is <code>PERMISSION_DENIED</code> and says nothing about
      why. A refusal of a credential of an active Project (a wrong secret, a
      revoked credential, or a call naming another Project) is recorded under
      the credential's own Project as <code>tenant_operator_refused</code>,
      with the <code>reason</code>. An unknown public id, another kind's
      credential and a suspended Project's record nothing.
    </li>
    <li>
      Calls that present it share one budget per client IP,
      <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_DIRECTORY_PER_IP</code> (default 120) per
      <code>GATEWAY_RATE_LIMIT_WINDOW_SECONDS</code> window, across the nine
      calls and across HTTP and native gRPC, so a guessed secret cannot be
      tried at line rate. Calls made with the admin secret alone are not
      counted. The setting is shared with the <code>LookupUsers</code>,
      <code>MatchTenantMemberPhones</code>, <code>TeammateKeyService</code>
      and <code>HireGrantService</code> budgets, which are counted separately:
      raising it raises those too.
    </li>
    <li>
      An empty <code>GATEWAY_ADMIN_API_SECRET</code> does not close these nine
      calls: they keep accepting every live <code>tenant_operator</code>
      credential. To cut the credentials off, revoke them with
      <code>AdminRevokeProjectCredential</code>, which takes the secret: set
      it again first if it was emptied.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    See <a href="/identity/docs/concepts/auth-model">Auth model</a> for how the Project
    and Tenant appear in JWT claims, and
    <a href="/identity/docs/installation/configuration">Configuration</a> for the full
    set of <code>GATEWAY_*</code> variables.
  </p>
